window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.6","title":"Expected Value and the Law of Large Numbers","goal":"Compute expected values for a die and for roulette, then watch the running average of simulated die rolls settle on 3.5 (the Law of Large Numbers).","question":"A casino patron asks two questions. If I roll a fair die many times, what number should my average settle on? And if I place a $1 bet on a single number at roulette (it pays $35 and has 1 chance in 38), do I win or lose money in the long run? Can the expected value answer both, and can we watch a simulation settle onto it?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Expected value of a die","text":"E(X) is each outcome times its probability, added up. A die never shows 3.5, but 3.5 is where the average of many rolls ends up.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nx = np.arange(1, 7)\np = np.full(6, 1 / 6)\n\nprint('x * P(x):', (x * p).round(4))\nprint('E(X) =', round((x * p).sum(), 4))","explain":["Load NumPy for arrays and fast arithmetic.","The outcomes of a die: the numbers 1 to 6.","Each outcome has probability 1/6. np.full repeats that value six times.","Print each outcome times its probability, the pieces of the expected value.","Add the pieces. The expected value is the sum of outcome times probability."],"meaning":"The six pieces are 0.1667, 0.3333, 0.5, 0.6667, 0.8333 and 1.0, and they add to E(X) = 3.5. A die never shows 3.5, but 3.5 is the long-run average of many rolls.","out":"x * P(x): [0.1667 0.3333 0.5    0.6667 0.8333 1.    ]\nE(X) = 3.5","figs":[]},{"title":"Is a bet worth taking?","text":"A $1 roulette bet on one number wins $35 with probability 1/38 and loses $1 otherwise. A negative expected value means a long-run loss for the player: the house edge.","code":"win, lose = 35, -1\np_win = 1 / 38\n\nev = win * p_win + lose * (1 - p_win)\nprint(f'expected gain per $1 bet = {ev:.4f}')\nprint(f'that is a loss of about {abs(ev) * 100:.1f} cents per dollar')","explain":["Win $35 or lose $1: the two possible outcomes of the roulette bet.","The chance of winning on one number is 1 in 38.","Expected gain: win times its probability plus loss times its probability.","Print the expected gain per $1 bet.","Convert it into cents of loss per dollar, so it is easy to understand."],"meaning":"The expected gain is -0.0526 dollars per $1 bet, a loss of about 5.3 cents per dollar. A negative expected value means the player loses on average in the long run, which is the house edge.","out":"expected gain per $1 bet = -0.0526\nthat is a loss of about 5.3 cents per dollar","figs":[]},{"title":"Simulate and track the running average","text":"We roll a die 5,000 times with a seeded generator. The running average after n rolls is the cumulative total divided by n. Early values jump around, later ones stay near 3.5.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nrolls = rng.integers(1, 7, size=5000)\nrunning = np.cumsum(rolls) / np.arange(1, 5001)\n\nfor n in [5, 50, 500, 5000]:\n    print(f'after {n:>4} rolls: average = {running[n - 1]:.3f}')","explain":["Create a seeded generator so the simulation repeats.","Roll a die 5,000 times.","Running average after n rolls: the cumulative total divided by the number of rolls so far.","Check the running average at a few chosen points.","Print each running average for rolls 5, 50, 500 and 5000."],"meaning":"After 5 rolls the average was 3.200, after 50 it was 3.760, after 500 it was 3.452 and after 5000 it was 3.487. The early values jump around, but they move toward the expected 3.5 as the rolls pile up. That is the Law of Large Numbers.","out":"after    5 rolls: average = 3.200\nafter   50 rolls: average = 3.760\nafter  500 rolls: average = 3.452\nafter 5000 rolls: average = 3.487","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the running average","text":"The line wobbles wildly at first and then flattens onto the expected value. Individual rolls stay random; the average becomes predictable.","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(np.arange(1, 5001), running, color='#2563eb', lw=1.2)\nplt.axhline(3.5, color='#dc2626', ls='--', label='E(X) = 3.5')\nplt.xscale('log')\nplt.xlabel('number of rolls (log scale)')\nplt.ylabel('running average')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","explain":["Load matplotlib, the plotting library.","Start a figure with a width and height that fit on a page.","Plot the running average against the number of rolls.","Add a dashed red line at the expected value 3.5 for comparison.","Use a log scale on the x-axis so the early rolls are not squashed.","Label the x-axis.","Label the y-axis.","Show the legend so the dashed line is named.","Display the figure."],"meaning":"The blue line wobbles wildly in the first few rolls and then flattens onto the dashed line at 3.5. Single rolls stay unpredictable, but the average of many becomes predictable.","out":"","figs":["2.6-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"x <- 1:6\nsum(x * 1/6)                  # E(X) of a die\n35 * (1/38) + (-1) * (37/38)  # roulette\n\nset.seed(42)\nrolls <- sample(6, 5000, replace = TRUE)\ncumsum(rolls)[c(5, 50, 500, 5000)] / c(5, 50, 500, 5000)","explain":["The six outcomes of a die.","Expected value: sum of outcome times 1/6.","Roulette: win $35 with probability 1/38, lose $1 with probability 37/38.","Fix the seed.","Roll the die 5,000 times, with replacement.","Running average at rolls 5, 50, 500 and 5000: running totals divided by the counts."],"expect":"3.5; -0.0526; running averages close to 3.5 by 5000 rolls (not identical to Python's)","read":"Check the first two results: 3.5 and -0.0526, which are exact and match Python. The last line gives four running averages that should drift toward 3.5 but will not be identical to Python's.","note":"The two expected values are exact and match Python. R draws different random rolls, so its running averages are close to 3.5 but not the same as Python's."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (expected values); Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives (the simulated mean)","syntax":"SET SEED=42.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP #i = 1 TO 5000.\nCOMPUTE roll = TRUNC(RV.UNIFORM(1, 7)).\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=roll.\nCOMPUTE ev_die = (1+2+3+4+5+6)/6.\nCOMPUTE roulette = 35/38 - 37/38.\nFORMATS ev_die roulette (F8.4).\nLIST VARIABLES=ev_die roulette /CASES=FROM 1 TO 1.","explain":["Fix the seed so the random numbers repeat.","Start a program that creates the data.","Loop 5,000 times.","Roll a die: RV.UNIFORM(1, 7) gives a number from 1 to 7 and TRUNC chops it to 1 to 6.","Write one row for each roll.","Close the loop.","No more rows.","End the program.","Ask for descriptive statistics, including the mean of the rolls.","Compute the expected value of a die.","Compute the expected gain of the roulette bet.","Show four decimals.","Print the two expected values from the first row."],"read":"In the Descriptive Statistics table, the Mean of roll should be close to 3.5. LIST shows ev_die 3.5000 and roulette -0.0526. The exact values match Python's; the simulated mean is close, not identical.","note":"Exact: 3.5 and -0.0526. The simulated mean of roll is close to 3.5 but not identical to Python's, and SPSS has no menu for a running-average plot."},"stata":{"code":"display (1+2+3+4+5+6)/6\ndisplay 35/38 - 37/38\n\nclear\nset seed 42\nset obs 5000\ngen roll = ceil(6*runiform())\ngen running = sum(roll) / _n\nlist running in 5\nlist running in 5000\nsummarize roll","explain":["The expected value of a die: Stata works as a calculator.","The expected gain of the roulette bet.","Clear memory before the simulation.","Fix the seed.","Make 5,000 empty rows, one per roll.","Roll a die: ceil(6*runiform()) turns a number between 0 and 1 into 1 to 6.","Running average: sum() is a running total, so dividing by _n (the row number) gives the running average.","Show the running average after 5 rolls.","Show the running average after 5,000 rolls.","Summarize the rolls, including the overall mean."],"read":"The first two lines print 3.5 and -.0526316, the exact values. The two list lines show the running average at roll 5 and at roll 5000, and the last summarize gives the mean of all rolls, near 3.5. The simulated values are close to Python's but not identical.","note":"The two display lines are exact (3.5 and -.0526316). In Stata sum() is a running total, so dividing by _n gives the running average. The simulated values are close to 3.5 but differ from Python's."},"conclusion":"The expected value of a die is 3.5, and the running average of 5,000 simulated rolls reached 3.487. The roulette bet has an expected value of -$0.0526 per $1, so a player loses about 5.3 cents per dollar in the long run. The caution: the expected value describes the long run, and a short run (even 50 rolls gave 3.760) can look very different.","practice":{"scenario":"At a school fair, a spinner game costs $3 to play. The spinner pays $10 with probability 0.1, pays $2 with probability 0.3, and pays nothing with probability 0.6. The organiser wants to know if the game makes money on average, and whether a simulation of many plays agrees.","data":"Prizes: [10, 2, 0] with probabilities [0.1, 0.3, 0.6]. Cost per play: 3. Simulate 20,000 plays with seed 5.","tasks":["Find the expected prize and the expected net gain per play for the player.","Simulate 20,000 plays and compute the running average of the net gain.","Report the running average after 10, 100 and 20,000 plays. What does it show?"],"hint":"Expected value = sum of prize x probability, then subtract the cost. For the simulation use rng.choice(prize, size=20000, p=prob) and np.cumsum divided by the play number.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\n\nprize = np.array([10, 2, 0])\nprob = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.6])\ncost = 3\n\nev_prize = (prize * prob).sum()\nprint(f'expected prize = {ev_prize:.2f}, expected net = {ev_prize - cost:.2f}')\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(5)\nplays = rng.choice(prize, size=20_000, p=prob) - cost\nrunning = np.cumsum(plays) / np.arange(1, 20_001)\nfor n in [10, 100, 20_000]:\n    print(f'after {n:>6,} plays: average net = {running[n - 1]:.3f}')","answer":"The expected prize is $1.60, so the expected net gain is $1.60 - $3 = -$1.40 per play (the organiser gains $1.40 per play). The running average of the player's net gain was 0.400 after 10 plays, -1.360 after 100 and -1.384 after 20,000. The long-run average settles close to -1.40, while the first 10 plays happened to look profitable.","out":"expected prize = 1.60, expected net = -1.40\nafter     10 plays: average net = 0.400\nafter    100 plays: average net = -1.360\nafter 20,000 plays: average net = -1.384","figs":[]}}});
